<Base title="About llmcpu" description="How the language-model CPU works, what the knobs do, and where the idea came from.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      A processor is a machine that fetches a few bytes, decides what they mean, does that, and moves on. This site
      replaces the part that decides with a language model. Everything else, memory, registers, the program counter,
      the console, the display, is ordinary and exact. The model is handed the bytes at the program counter and a small
      set of tools: read memory, write memory, read a register, write a register, do one piece of 32-bit arithmetic,
      print, light a pixel, and move the program counter. Moving the program counter is what finishes an instruction.
    </p>
    <p>
      A silicon processor has one fixed idea of what bytes mean, and stops dead at the first word that does not fit
      it. The model has no such idea unless you give it one. By default it is told only that the bytes are a program in
      a language it alone knows. So a poem is a program. So is a photograph, or a font, or noise. The question the site
      asks is not whether the model gets it right, because there is no right, but what it makes of what it is given,
      and how it fails: not by halting, but by ploughing on, one token at a time, into readings nobody designed.
    </p>

    <h2>Step zero: the language</h2>
    <p>
      Before anything runs, the model is shown the first 64 bytes of memory, as hex and as text, and asked to design
      the language they are written in: how many bytes make an instruction or what marks where one ends, how the
      pieces of an instruction decide what it does, what the registers and memory are for, and what the program prints
      or draws. That design is shown back to it on every instruction and rendered on the page as its own pane. It is
      held to it in two ways. The model can change its mind, but only with a <code>revise</code> op that rewrites one
      part of the design out loud, and every revision shows in the trace. And the machine checks the one rule the
      free reading has, that the same bytes mean the same thing: an instruction whose bytes were read before with a
      different effect is flagged. Whether it reads whole lines, words, four-byte words or single bytes is its call;
      the echo shows 64 bytes so the chunk is not an artefact of what it was shown.
    </p>

    <h2>What to watch for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Echo</strong> is the easy reading of text: an instruction is a line, its meaning is to print itself. The
        larger models settle into it quickly. It is the least interesting thing a model can do here, and the site is
        arranged so that it is not the only thing you see.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Self-modification.</strong> When the model stores into the bytes it is reading, the program is
        rewriting itself as it runs. The count of rewritten bytes is a headline measure, the bytes are marked in the
        text and memory panes, and a diff against the original image is a click away. A small model once overwrote a
        poem with a bytecode of its own invention.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The display.</strong> Printing lets a model echo; a picture does not. What does a poem draw? What does
        a JPEG draw when it is read as instructions rather than decoded?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drift.</strong> A language designed for the first 64 bytes meets bytes it did not anticipate. Does the
        model revise, or read differently without saying so, or wander into the console page and print it?
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Recordings</h2>
    <p>
      A run of a real model takes seconds per instruction and a GPU with a few gigabytes of memory, which most laptops
      do not have. So the model dropdown starts with recordings: complete traces of real runs, with the model's design
      step, every round of every instruction, its comments and its tool calls, replayed through the same interface at
      your pace. Nothing in a recording is edited or synthesised, and every recording is labelled with the model, the
      knobs, the date and the hardware. A banner over the machine says when you are watching one. The live models are
      the bonus for those with the hardware.
    </p>

    <h2>The machine</h2>
    <p>
      It is a 32-bit RISC-V machine with the base integer instruction set only (RV32I, about forty instructions) and
      16 KiB of memory. Three devices are mapped above memory: a console page at <code>0x4000</code>, where a store
      prints the bytes stored; a halt port at <code>0x4100</code>; and a 32 by 32 pixel display at
      <code>0x5000</code>, one byte per pixel, the low four bits picking one of sixteen colours. The curated programs
      are small C programs compiled with clang for exactly this machine, with no standard library; their sources,
      binaries and line tables are in the
      <a href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/llmcpu/tree/main/programs">programs directory</a>. The colours running
      through the source, assembly, memory and trace panes follow the compiler's own line table, the same trick
      <a href="https://godbolt.org">Compiler Explorer</a> uses to show which line became which instructions. For a
      RISC-V program you can ask for a second, silicon processor to run the same bytes alongside and see exactly where
      the model's reading parts from the official one.
    </p>

    <h2>The model</h2>
    <p>
      Models run entirely in your browser through <a href="https://github.com/mlc-ai/web-llm">WebLLM</a> on WebGPU.
      Nothing you load, paste or drop leaves your machine. The dropdown lists every small Qwen the runtime ships
      prebuilt, plus a couple of other families for comparison; the first load downloads the weights, which the browser
      then caches. The model's replies are constrained to a JSON grammar, so it physically cannot produce a malformed
      tool call, only a wrong one. Every reply also carries a one-sentence comment, which is the narration you see in
      the trace.
    </p>
    <p>
      The perfect oracle in the same dropdown is not a language model. It is a silicon RISC-V processor answering in
      the model's place: it reads RISC-V and nothing else, so on a poem it shrugs and steps over every four bytes, and
      its language design is always the RISC-V manual. It exists so that the machinery can be seen working on a device
      without WebGPU.
    </p>

    <h2>The knobs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>How to read the bytes</strong> is the main one. The default tells the model nothing about what an
        instruction is: it designs a language and reads in it. The other three settings tell it the bytes are RISC-V,
        with increasing help: raw words only, a compact manual, or the manual plus each instruction already decoded
        for it. They form a ladder from invention to execution.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>State shown each step</strong> sets how much of the machine the model sees without asking: just the
        program counter and the bytes ahead of it, or the non-zero registers too, or nearby memory and the console as
        well. The rest it has to fetch with tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Thinking before acting</strong> gives the model a scratchpad of a few sentences or a paragraph before it
        commits to its tool calls.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Instructions remembered</strong> is how many of its own recent steps the model is reminded of. Together
        with its language design and its note, it is the model's only memory of what happened before; the machine's
        state is the rest.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Prior art</h2>
    <p>
      In 2023 Brad Grantham had GPT-4 <a href="https://bradgrantham.github.io/gpt-4-6502/">single-step a 6502 FizzBuzz</a>,
      one instruction per prompt with a line of reasoning before each state update; it got 286 of 288 steps right.
      Jonas Degrave's <a href="https://www.engraved.blog/building-a-virtual-machine-inside/">virtual machine inside
      ChatGPT</a> (2022) is the famous case of a model sustaining a convincing computer by narration alone, and the
      <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/vJFdjigzmcXMhNTsx/simulators">simulators</a> framing from the same year
      is the vocabulary for why a model will play out whatever process it is conditioned on, halting or not.
      Formally, prompting can make a transformer
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.14310">execute loops step by step</a> and is even
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01992">Turing complete</a> given a scratchpad; empirically, benchmarks find
      that models <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14777">execute state machines over long horizons</a> badly
      unless the state is externalised at every step, which is what the tools here do. The nearest thing to a model
      reading raw bytes as a machine is <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.19155">bGPT</a>, a byte-level model trained
      to simulate a CPU from its binary; the difference here is that a general chat model was never trained for any of
      this, and is asked to read bytes that were never a program at all. That last move has its own lineage in
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Databending">databending</a>, where a file is opened in software meant for
      another kind, and in <a href="https://esolangs.org/wiki/Piet">Piet</a>, where the program is a picture. The
      memory-mapped display and the register pane owe their shape to
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CHIP-8">CHIP-8</a> and the tradition of tiny teaching machines.
    </p>

